pui ming Wong wrote:
> 
> Edwige et Denis Fournier (dfournier@teaser.fr) wrote:
> : >Any pecularities between aix and tru64 unix ?
> : >I placed both the ip nos and hostnames of an aix and
> : >a tru64 machine in another tru64's .rhosts file
> 
> : did you put the hostname of all machine in /etc/hosts ?
> 
> : Denis
> Made it work afterwards. That aix box has 2 ethernets of which
> one of them is in the same subnet as that of the tru64
> I placed that ip address of the aix in the tru64 /.rhosts
> instead of the other ip (the one which is not of the same
> subnet), and rsh worked
> Dunno why,though !
> Anyone knows ?

Routing, if you haven't explicitly set up a route to cross subnets then
the default is to route via the interface in the same subnet as the
system you want to talk to.

Whenever you have authentications problems it's best to do the following
on the systems involved and for all ip addresses involved:

1)  check name resolution
    do a 'host <hostname>' to check resolution from /etc/hosts.
    do a 'nslookup <hostname>' to check resolution from DNS.
    check name resolution configuration files /etc/hosts,
/etc/resolv.conf & /etc/netsvc.conf, also check to see if environment
variable NSORDER is set.
    change configuration if required, repeat tests.

2)  check the routing
    do a 'ping -c 1 <hostname>' to see if there is a communications
path.
    do a 'traceroute <hostname>' to find out the path communication will
take.
    do a 'netstat -rn' to see what routes are present.
    do a 'netstat -in' to confirm that the ip addresses are on the
interface you think they are.
    change, add, delete routes if required, repeat tests.

3)  double-check that you've set up the authentication configuration
correctly.

-- 
Regards,
Clive



