Customer _and_ shareholder.  I don't have 6-figures shares, and my company
sells in the two-figure range of systems each year, and we don't sell Compaq
wintel (only Alpha/VMS) so honestly Compaq doesn't give a flying fudge about
us or our customers, and doubtless rather wishes we'd go away or just
conform to their nice little wintel-centric view of the way things ought to
be.  Speaking as a reseller (used to be ASP) and user since the mid 1980s
(the company connection goes back to the early 1970s, before my time)
Digital itself never got as bad, as totally full of itself and its own view
of what should be, _even_ under GQ Palmer, as it is now under current
management.

I'm voting my meager irrelevant shares against the merger.  If I had any way
of getting to the meetings I'd try to speak towards removal of the current Q
management on the basis of their complicity in brain dead marketing (or
outright lack of marketing) of enterprise systems, shabby treatment of
customers trying to buy or get information on non-wintel Q systems,
devolution of what once was a world class service organization to the shell
that remains (I've got a couple dozen customers with soft and hardware
service contracts; every single one has noted large increases in response
time, and call takers utterly clueless on VMS systems, at the least!), with
the resulting frittering away of many customers and losses to stockholder
value.  Pfeiffer was let go for a lot less than this.  Its time for a clean
sweep; it'd be hard to do worse than this bunch has done the last couple of
years.  And yes, its in part because I do have a personal stake in this; VMS
is my chosen career and I believe my best chance of staying in that career
is a Compaq with new, competent, management not under the thumb of the borg
of redmond (I can hope...)

I'm also voting my stake in HP against the merger; in fact I decided to send
in the green proxy to Mr. Hewlett in case any other issues came up because
I'm satisfied that long term shareholder value would be better served by
staying the hell away from Compaq and the management that dragged it to its
current state.

Rich Jordan






