Terry,

I'm not sure this deal really brings anything to the table that Compaq,
focusing intensely hard for the next 2 years on decent marketing and
intelligent decision making, wouldn't bring. And I'm not sure that the HP
'brain trust' is capable of making the intelligent decisions required in a
merged company either.

This past year has been more than a writeoff - it's been a big step
backwards for Compaq on many fronts despite the finanical performance.

I has been my experience, having lived through 2 comparatively large mergers
on the 'winning side' (where both parties were roughly equal in size and
market share), that the dust really doesn't settle for a good 12-15 months
no matter what anyone says. Too much jockeying for position, turf defending,
back-stabbing, and the inevitable delays and operational screw-ups that go
on irrespective of what senior management wants or thinks is happening. The
financial benefits don't really show themselves until the third year.

Compaq has a good enough product portfolio, across almost all lines of
business, that all it really needs is better execution and marketing. All
the money and time that has been wasted, and time and money that will be
wasted through this merger would have been better spent promoting Compaq's
products and services.

All the stuff there will be economies of scale are mostly BS - Compaq & HP
are both large enough that they'd be hard pressed to extract more than an
extra 1-2%  concession from their suppliers, at wholesale prices, for being
a single customer. Yes there will be some marketing money saved, but not
that much, perhaps $100MM per year, as they get the 'combined message out in
extra spending. The salary savings will be much less than is surmised - many
non-US jurisdictions have far more stringent employee termination
compensation rules that in the USA. Many expensive office leases will have
to be broken, many assets will be sold at less than their fair market value
just to get rid of them for political expediency (seen it happen before).
This is a very costly exercise, and while all this is going on, Sun, IBM, &
Dell will be looking to pick-off every disgruntled customer there is, with
greater success than the Carly/Curls duo will care to admit.

Lets assume that Enterprise products and Services have been the profitable
side of Compaq (and I think that most in c.o.v. will concede that this is
true). Then we have to look at WHY this is so - what are the product
differentiators that make customers buy these products over those of the
competition. All of us here think we know the answer - high quality
operating systems, and a processor that brings significant cost/tp
advantages or parity under most circumstances (I know that this will
generate some howls of protest, but many $/TPC benchmarks show this).

Against this we have a smaller 3rd party application pool to draw from,
inane management and marketing (or lack thereof), flip-flops, and customer
FUD.

When I look at the con's in the previous sentence, all of this is derived
from one fundamental source - incompetent leadership at the highest levels.


I can distinguish between the need for OpenVMS and the desire for Alpha. To
have Alpha and OpenVMS is the best of both worlds, but when push comes to
shove, I'd pick OpenVMS over Alpha. The question remains, is there enough of
an advantage (economic, performance, or comfort-level) among current and
potential OpenVMS customers to moving to Itanic/McKinley? Will this move
bring enough NEW revenue in to have warranted the transition to what is
arguably a lesser performing architecture?


I think I'd have a lot more confidence if Compaq stayed independent, the
Board was fired, and Lou Gerstner was brought in to run the place.




