"John Smith" claimed:
> If the merger is voted down, it's important that HP shareholders vote NO,
> and Compaq shareholders vote yes. In this way, the deal 'breakup fee' of
> $675 million gets paid to Compaq by HP, and not the other way around.

The $675M penalty does NOT happen if some side's shareholders vote no.
This penalty clause of the merger agreement can be triggered only by the
actions of boards of directors, and of individual top executives, and of
committees that directors or executives establish, if they work on
competing merger deals or work against this deal.  The contract is online
at the Compaq merger info page.

It would be nice for Compaq (and bad for HP) if HP director Walter
Hewlett's public actions could be labelled as a penalty-triggering event.
If any executive of HP did what he did, the penalty would certainly apply.
But I gather that Walter's actions "as a shareholder" do not.  Nor do
anti-merger votes of a minority of individual directors count, whenever the
board votes as a group pro-merger on merger matters.  For directors,
the penalty is triggered only if the board votes as a whole anti-merger,
or if some director works (as a director) against this deal or works on
competing deals.

If the merger is voted down and Compaq feels testy about it, it will very
interesting to see if Compaq tries to get paid the penalty under the theory
that Walter's aggressive anti-merger actions were done as a director,
not as some mere shareholder.  E.g. does Walter's anti-merger campaign
literature mention Walter's credentials as a sitting long-time director,
or carefully omits that detail?  It may well be that Walter should have
resigned from the board, the day he began doing press announcements.

The apparent purpose of this kind of contract clause in agreements-
to-merge, is to reduce the mischief from internal politics and of
jockeying for bigger bids from additional suitors.  But as a bad side
effect, it squelches any rational public discussion of "Plan B's" until
the shareholder vote is taken, and probably hinders full contingency
planning in case the deal fails to get regulatory & shareholder approvals.

I haven't yet decided which way to vote my Compaq shares.
Compaq's surviving enterprise system products would be much
easier to market under the HP brand name than under the
PC-only Compaq brand.  The melded OS product lines could be
stronger than now if customers get good migration support.
But can these managers manage this thing, can the surviving
employees become enrolled in the new company, and can
the pace of product development be kept  competitive?
Compaq did a rotten job of digesting the Digital acquisition.

   -- Duane Sand, not speaking for Compaq, HP, etc.








