Bob Ceculski wrote:
> if no merger, why would they kill their number one money maker?
> if anything, they would need it more than ever ...

It isn't a question of killing it. It is a question of focusing on the wintel
side of business to become a lean and mean PC maker. Those legacy products can
remain hidden in the basement and fed just enough rations to stay alive as
long as they don't make any noise or cause problems.

However, instead of simplyfying its architectures, by killing Alpha, Compaq
serously made it far more complex:

Tandem-Mips
Tandem-IA64
VMS-VAX
VMS-Alpha
VMS-IA64
Tru64-Alpha (possibly also Tru64 IA64)
Linux-Alpha
Linux-8086
Linux-IA64
Windows-8086
Windows-IA64
Wince-Strongarm

HP only adds 3 to the list: HP-UX PA-risc, HP-UX IA64 and MPE 

Now, from an HP point of view, if they inherit all that, it means that they
must support all of those for a number of years to come, especially since IA64
will take another few years before it is respectable enough to be sold to
serious customers.  The temptation to streamline the product list will be
great. The temptation to announce end of life for some products ASAp will be
great so that even though full produc support will continue for many years,
Carly can claim to have taken the necessary actions to streamline the product portfolio.

I could imagine an "Alpha like" scenario where Carly would announce the
donation of VMS and half its engineers to Microsoft on March 21st, and that
VMS would continue to be developped until version 8, at which piont it is
expected that Windows will have incorporated all the functions that will allow
VMS customers to migrate to Unix or Windows.


