> If you get this information for every file in a fileset's name space,
> then you will be able to build a map of what 512 byte sectors on the
> disk contain file data.
> 
> If you have multiple filesets, then the files in each fileset's name
> space will be intermixed in the domain.  There is not attempt to order
> or place files from one fileset differently than another fileset's
> files.  Files get storage allocated to them from the domain on a first
> come first served basis.
> 
> X  ................................................................ My
> X  intent is to get the starting offset of the fileset extents and the
> X  size of the extents. Can this be accomplished by the fcntl call(with
> X  F_GETMAP). ...........................................................
> 
> No, I do not think so.  fcntl(F_GETMAP) does not tell you the physical
> location of a file's extents on the disk.  It just tells you what parts
> of the file have storage allocated and what parts are sparse holes.  So
> you would need to use something like showfile -x to get physical
> locations of extents on the disk for each file. 
> 
> X  .......... The problem is that the fcntl call will not account for the
> X  advfs fileset as a whole and just work for the UFS directory which is
> X  the mountpoint. ......................................................
> 
> _NOTHING_ shows all the storage associated with a fileset as a whole. 
> fcntl(F_GETMAP) is a per file utility. If you point it at the mount
> point, then it tells you about the root directory of that mounted file
> systems.  If you want fcntl(F_GETMAP) information for every file in a
> file system, then you need to call fcntl(F_GETMAP) once for every file
> in the file system.
> 
> But again, fcntl(F_GETMAP) does not tell you where extents are located
> on the physical disk, it just tells you about what parts of a file have
> storage and what parts have sparse holes.
>   
> X  ............... It would be great if you could clarify on the showfile
> X  and vbmtpg.
> 
> Showfile -x is rather straight forward.  It shows you the allocated
> storatge of a file, offset of the extent as an 8K page inside of the
> file (offsets that are not covered by an extent are sparse holes), and
> it shows where the extent lives in the physical domain.  Not much more
> to tell.
> 
> As for the old out dated vbmtpg, it was replaced by nvbmtpg in newer
> versions of Tru64 UNIX and both of them along with the other tools in
> /sbin/advfs/* are non-trivial tools for analyzing the AdvFS on-disk
> structures.  What public information is available on them is in the man
> pages.  After that, it is a matter of either reverse engineering what
> you see, or getting a source license and studying the sources. 
> 
> But if you are still looking for a magic tool that will tell you what
> subset of storage is being used by all the files described by a single
> fileset's name space, then you are not going to find it (it is not the
> kind of information that is interesting to most people, especially
> AdvFS developers), so you will need to write you own tool.
> 
> Here is a rude, crude, and slow script (which is _NOT_ very well
> debugged and will most likely break) that will find the high and low
> blocks used by a given fileset:
> 
> #!/bin/ksh
> #
> if [[ $# = 0 ]]; then
>     echo 1>&2 "Usage:  fileset_lo_hi_allocation.ksh mount_point"
>     exit 1
> fi
> 
> find $1 -mount -exec showfile -x {} \; | awk '
>     $1 == pageOff { next }
>     NF == 5 {
>         if ( vol < $3 )
>             vol = $3
>         if ( lo[$3] > $4 || lo[$3] == 0 )
>             lo[$3] = $4
>         n = $4 + $5 - 1
>         if ( hi[$3] < n )
>             hi[$3] = n
>     }
>     END {
>         for(j=1; j <= vol; j++ ) {
>             if ( j in lo ) {
>                 printf("vol:%d lo:%d hi:%d\n",
>                     j, lo[j], hi[j])
>             }
>         }
>     }'  # don't miss the closing single quote or not work.
> 
> This is _NOT_ what you asked for, but it does show using the showfile
> -x command in a script.  You can copy the algorithm into another
> language and use different tools and you can expand on the algorithm to
> do what you want.  
> 
> I'm just not sure what you want makes any sense (at least not with
> respect to an AdvFS domain).  Do you have an overall goal in mind.  A
> "Big Picture" if you will.
> 
>                                         Bob Harris
