> No, sorry a clone does not work the way you seem to be thinking.  A
> clone starts by marking the original fileset so that it knows it has
> been cloned, then it duplicates the original fileset's tag directory
> (I've mentioned this before; it provides tranlations between bitfile
> tag ID's (like an inode) to the primary mcell location for each file in
> the fileset name space.
> 
> From this point forward, if there is a change to any file, the clone
> fileset allocates a new primary mcell for the file (on the first
> modification of a file), and puts that the clone file's primary mcell
> the clone's tag directory file.  Then just enough new storage is
> allocated for the clone to equal the amount of storage being modified,
> and the original data being modified is copied to the new storage in
> the clone (copy-on-write or COW), then the original file's storage is
> modified.  An original file being deleted or truncated, just has all of
> its original storage moved directly into the clone (after all it
> doesn't need it any more).
> 
> An empty clone fileset just contains a tag directory and some state
> information on the original fileset to say there is a clone.
> 
> There is no need for the clone to know anything more about the files in
> the original fileset, nor about what storage those files use until the
> original files start to be modified.
> 
> When the clone is mounted and then read (such as during a backup),
> since it has a copy of the original's tag directory, it can find all
> the unmodified data of the files in the original fileset name space. 
> And for modified files, the clone has its own tracking primary mcell
> that keeps track of the storage that was modified in the original,
> otherwise, the clone uses the original file's mcell information to read
> the unmodified storage of the modified file.
> 
> Removing a clone consists of walking the clone's tag directory file and
> deleting all the storage and mcells that were allocated to the clone
> because of modifications to the original fileset.  A clone that
> experienced very few copy-on-write operations will have very little to
> delete and will be rmfset'ed very quickly.  A clone that has had lots
> of copy-on-write operations performed to it, will take much longer to
> rmfset.
> 
> Now I've been saying that a fileset is just a name space.  If you think
> about the fact that a clone fileset starts out life as just a name
> space that points to the same files as it original, and from a read
> perspective, the file does not care whether it is read by the original
> fileset or by the cloned fileset.  So a name space is like your address
> book.  It is just a pointer to the real thing, and the real think for a
> file is its metadata and storage kept in the domain, _NOT_ in the
> fileset which is just an address book that points to the file.  And a
> cloned fileset is just a copy of the original address book.
> 
> Once modifications start to happen, the 2 address books get marked up
> and start to diverge, but the fact still remains that a fileset just
> keeps track of the names (actually the bitfile tag numbers) of files it
> is tracking and where they are located.  Nothing more.
> 
> X  fileset. It would be great if you could let me know.
> 
> What you are looking for does not exist.  There is no need for it to
> exist.  It has no value to AdvFS, so there is no need to track that
> information and no need to build an interface to get it.
