I said my script was rude, crude, and not very well debugged.  All that
it did was to find the lowest and highest allocated block used by file
data.  The script was nothing more.  At the time that seems to be what
you were interested in.

Since you have not told us what your goal is, there is no way I would
attempt to write a script to solve it.  So what is your goal?  What is
it you are trying to do? 

And back to the fileset.  Again, the fileset is just a name space and
consists of a tag directory that keeps track of bitfile tag IDs (like
an inode number) and points at the primary mcell of each file known to
the fileset.  The file itself may be part of the original fileset and
it may be part of the clone fileset, so the file itself is not
dedicated to a fileset, but rather an entity pointed to by a fileset.

All the other metadata is part of the domain and is shared by _ALL_
filesets in the domain.

The utility /sbin/advfs/vdf can be used to display the amount of space
being used by metadata.  There should be a man page for vdf.

                                        Bob Harris


